      "reason": "Forbes and scholarly local biography support each fact; identity confidence bounds the pair."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2"
    ],
    "word_count": 51,
    "html": "<p>Melvin Simon was an American property developer who built a billionaire fortune through shopping centres. Born in Brooklyn, he settled in Indianapolis after Army service and worked as a leasing agent. With his brothers, he founded the business that became Simon Property Group, helping expand enclosed malls across the United States.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Melvin Simon helped make the suburban mall a repeatable business model. His company secured department-store tenants on favourable rents before choosing development sites, giving banks greater confidence to finance construction. Smaller shops paid higher rents, sometimes with an additional charge tied to sales. The arrangement supported the expansion of Simon Property Group and placed large retailers at the centre of its developments.\n\nWith his wife, Bren, Simon also directed substantial giving towards cancer care and research in Indianapolis. Their gift to Indiana University helped expand patient facilities and created an endowment in memory of their son, Joshua Max. The fund supported research and the recruitment of faculty, alongside the treatment space financed by the same donation.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 87,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Local encyclopedia explains specific financing mechanism; university recipient explains uses and memorial purpose of joint gift."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S2",
      "S3"
    ],
    "word_count": 116,
    "html": "<p>Melvin Simon helped make the suburban mall a repeatable business model. His company secured department-store tenants on favourable rents before choosing development sites, giving banks greater confidence to finance construction. Smaller shops paid higher rents, sometimes with an additional charge tied to sales. The arrangement supported the expansion of Simon Property Group and placed large retailers at the centre of its developments.</p>\r\n<p>With his wife, Bren, Simon also directed substantial giving towards cancer care and research in Indianapolis. Their gift to Indiana University helped expand patient facilities and created an endowment in memory of their son, Joshua Max. The fund supported research and the recruitment of faculty, alongside the treatment space financed by the same donation.</p>\r\n"
  },
